  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday morning through next Monday)\nIssued at 104 PM EST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nBeginning Wednesday night into Thursday morning the front will be \npushing into the NW and pushing through northern Georgia. Thankfully \nas this line pushes through it will be during the overnight and well \ndisplaced from the parent low meaning we are unlikely to see any \nstrong or severe storms. With the lack of any blocking features, the \nfront will continue to progress through the northern and \nnortheastern portions of the state into early Thursday morning with \nminimal precip expected. Rainfall totals are expected to be between \n0.10-0.25\" during this time frame with no flooding expected to \noccur. Following the frontal passage the overall synoptic pattern \nwill continue to showcase a broad trough over the eastern CONUS \nwhich may contribute to gustier winds on Friday in conjunction with \na post frontal environment. Current guidance suggests wind gusts \nbetween 20-25mph but on the higher end could see gusts as high as 30-\n35mph through Friday afternoon. Will need to keep an eye on our \nmountain counties as those areas will likely see slightly higher \nwinds that may approach wind advisory criteria. Following the \nfrontal passage and into the weekend, the coldest air of the season \nwill begin to impact much of north and central Georgia. Highs this \nweekend will be in the low to mid 50s across the area before slowly \nwarming into next week into the low 60s for highs. Lows on the other \nhand will dip to around or below freezing for much of the area \nbeginning Friday night into Saturday morning and then again Saturday \nnight into Sunday morning. For some areas in north Georgia expected \nto reach below 28 this could be the \"killing\" freeze for \nagricultural needs. Pushing past the weekend another wave looks to \nmove in early next week which will bring our next chance for \nrainfall but uncertainty remains on timing and amounts.",
